[Music]  hello and welcome to how the light gets  in festival i'm grace blakely author of  the corona crush how the pandemic will  change capitalism and stolen how to save  the world from financialization and  today i'm going to be interviewing the  brilliant rooker bregman author of uh  humankind a hopeful history i'm going to  be talking about that book humankind  today which i would really encourage  everyone to purchase and read so the  first question to you ruka  is for all the talk of solidarity and  elevating our common humanity why do you  think that so many people have such a  hard time believing that all human  beings are basically  yeah that's really the central question  isn't it um if people are really  fundamentally decent then uh yeah why do  so many of us believe that we're  basically totally selfish  and you know what i've got i've got a  very long answer to that  because i basically think there are four  reasons and i try to go through them  quickly so the first one is relatively  straightforward right it's uh  it's the news it's the kind of  information that we get every day the  news is mostly about stuff that goes  wrong about corruption and terrorism and  violence so if you watch a lot of the  news then at the end of the day you'll  be quite cynical and you get what  psychologists call the mean world  syndrome you know this this feeling that  the world is just a nasty and evil place  so perhaps it's uh it's good for your  mental health to just follow a little  bit less of the news but then obviously  i mean the news is a relatively modern  phenomenon and um  this  cynical view of human nature has been  with us for a very long time i think you  could even argue that it's one of the  central ideas in western culture this  idea that people deep down are just  selfish and that our civilization is  only a thin veneer you know only a thin  layer and below that lies raw human  nature it goes back all the way to the  ancient greeks you can find it among the  orthodox christians you know saint  augustine uh writing about the notion of  original sin that we're all sinners you  can find it among the enlightenment  philosophers among social darwinists in  the 19th century it's embedded at the  heart of capitalism again this idea that  people are just selfish and greed is  good etc etc so from the left to the  right from from people who are religious  to atheists this idea comes back again  and again and again  and um  sorry this is a way too long answer but  just one final thing to say is that the  reason  i think it comes back again and again  and again  is that it's in the interest of those in  power  because  if we cannot trust each other  then we need  those at the top  then we need hierarchy um we need kings  and queens and presidents and ceos and  you name it uh basically we need uh a  leviathan as thomas hopps the the famous  british philosopher called it someone to  keep us in check because otherwise we  start looting and plundering and killing  each other and we we  behave as the kids in lord of the flies  so it's um this cynical selfish view of  human nature is is i think the ultimate  legitimization of power differences in  hierarchy and that's why this book  humankind may come across as this uh i  don't know self-helpy book about oh the  power of kindness  but i think it's actually a quite  subversive political book  if you  really get what it's about um  you know changing our view of human  nature it has quite revolutionary  implications  yeah absolutely and i think certainly  with the kind of you know revival of  this idea of democratic socialism that  we're seeing in lots of parts of the  world that's really premised upon the  idea that we are capable of cooperating  within institutions within democratic  institutions to actually make the world  a better place so i completely agree  that this is really radical political  stuff  um you mentioned that the idea that you  know if we were all left wearing devices  it would end up like lord of the flies  right but in your book you have a really  fascinating anecdote about the real lord  of the flies it's more than an anger  actually it's an incredible story can  you just um tell us a little bit about  that story and the kind of research that  you did to find out about it sure well  many of us will probably have read lord  of the flies i uh read it when i was i  think around 16 or 17 years old and i  remember feeling quite depressed  afterwards um  like uh no more harry potter for me this  is what kids are really like that's what  i thought uh it was only years later  while i was working on this project that  i wondered  um  could lord of the flies just be you know  another version of this veneer theory  you know this this deep uh deeply  influential idea in western culture um  and has everyone  ever checked what would really happen if  kids would ship wreck on an island  now  obviously it's uh it's hard for  scientists to convince parents to send  their kids to uninhabited islands right  so it's hard to test this scientifically  what would happen but you could look at  natural experiments so i just  basically just started googling right  whether  it has ever happened um and then i  stumbled upon this obscure blog that  told a story about something that had  supposedly happened in the 60s  near tonga which is an island group in  the pacific ocean where supposedly six  kids had shipwrecked  and then i devoted uh around a year of  my life to this story  because uh i managed to track down the  original lord of the flies children um  six stunwin boys  who are now around 17 years old four of  them are still alive um they're around  70 years old now and  they did shipwreck on an island and they  behaved in a very very different way uh  from from the lord of the flies children  it's a it's a quite hopeful story if it  would be a hollywood film then people  would say oh this is highly unrealistic  you know kids would never cooperate like  that but you know it really happened  personally i think that it should be a  hollywood film maybe that's one for your  next project  well it is it is going to be so they've  great yeah yeah so we've we formed a  team you know the fourth survivor  and and the captain who found them who  sadly passed away a couple of weeks ago  his name is peter warner um so yeah we  together we sold the rights to a new  regency they've also  done 12 years a slave and bohemian  rhapsody so now it's going to be this  huge  production uh it's been a it's been  absolutely yeah it's been a crazy  experiment experience but you know it's  just one wonderful story and i think  that if if kids from around the globe  still have to read lord of the flies in  school today which is which is fine i  think it's an interesting novel and it  you know ask some important questions um  but then please let's also tell them  about this one time that we know of in  all of world history where real kids  shipwrecked on a real island because  that's a completely totally different  story of of kids who cooperated for 15  months  and um who who stayed the best of  friends basically one of my favorite  parts of the book and um the you know  the title of the episode of when i had  you on my podcast that i used was this  idea of survival of the friendliest um i  particularly really found those studies  that demonstrated that the thing that  really differentiates human beings from  other animals isn't necessarily  massive you know levels of intelligence  but actually sociality can you explain  this idea of survival of the friendliest  and why it's so important for kind of  explaining why human beings have this  natural capacity to kind of work  together to solve their collective  problems for a long time evolutionary  psychology told this quite  cynical story about selfish genes  and that perhaps we just need to teach  our kids generosity and altruism because  they are born selfish if you studied  biology in the 1970s that was basically  uh the story that your teachers would  tell you  um but there's really been a scientific  revolution or a sort of silent  scientific revolution in the last couple  of decades so many experts from very  different disciplines have moved on to a  more hopeful view of human nature and so  biologists have indeed come to talk  about this whole concept of survival of  the friendliest which basically means  that for millennia it was not the  nastiest among us or the smartest among  us but actually the friendliest who had  the most kids and still had the biggest  chance of passing on their genes to the  next generation  now the question is obviously how do we  know this  um  there's a there's a there's a name for  this uh theory they call it  self-domestication  um i guess we we all know what  domesticated animals are we've got cows  we've got pigs and and sheep etc  and we know that for a long time we've  selected these animals for tameness and  friendliness and  biologists have known for a very long  time that if you domesticate an animal  you start seeing this list of of traits  so domesticated animals have thinner  bones uh smaller brains they're much  more playful it's as if they never  really want to grow up um  and this whole list of traits together  it's called domestication syndrome and  what's really interesting about human  beings if that is that if you look at  this list you start to think but wait a  minute humans are quite domesticated  right we are very slow to grow up you  know some people never grow up we are  very playful especially when when we're  young we have smaller brains compared to  our ancestors if you compare homo  sapiens skeletons from 50 40 30 20 000  years ago um you see that difference um  so in many ways it seems to be the case  that we are domesticated but then the  question is who did it you know who  tamed us who has domesticated us and the  answer that scientists now give is  to continue watching this